# CODEOWNERS - Spec-Lock-Diff, Control 5A (protected paths)
#
# Copy this file to .github/CODEOWNERS (or CODEOWNERS at the repository root)
# and replace @your-org/data-platform with the team that must approve.
# Then turn on branch protection with "Require review from Code Owners".
#
# What this file does: git refuses to merge a pull request that touches one of
# these paths until a human owner approves it. It is a control, not a rule -
# it works whether or not the agent read AGENTS.md.
#
# One line per row of the README's Control 5A table, in the same order.

# CI workflows. If the agent changes the CI, it controls the rules.
/.github/                       @your-org/data-platform

# Local validation hooks.
/.pre-commit-config.yaml        @your-org/data-platform

# The file that defines who approves what. Both places git looks for it.
/.github/CODEOWNERS             @your-org/data-platform
/CODEOWNERS                     @your-org/data-platform

# The agent's rules.
/AGENTS.md                      @your-org/data-platform

# dbt dependencies. An agent could pin a vulnerable version. The README's row is
# packages.yml; the lock file and dependencies.yml are the same decision under
# other names, and gate rule G6 watches all three.
/packages.yml                   @your-org/data-platform
/package-lock.yml               @your-org/data-platform
/dependencies.yml               @your-org/data-platform

# The version of these tools the CI installs, if you install rather than vendor.
# It is the same kind of decision as a package pin, and it is what makes a
# downgrade of the gate a change somebody approves rather than one line in a diff.
/.slp-version                   @your-org/data-platform

# Global project configuration.
/dbt_project.yml                @your-org/data-platform

# Macros are reused by several models. One change affects everything.
/macros/                        @your-org/data-platform

# Generic tests.
/tests/                         @your-org/data-platform

# Reconciliation queries. If the agent changes the reconciliation in the same
# PR as the model, it controls what is being verified.
/analyses/reconciliation_*      @your-org/data-platform

# Metric definitions. A wrong metric propagates errors to all consumers.
/models/semantic/               @your-org/data-platform

# Statistical profiles. If the agent changes the profile, it changes its own
# reference.
/docs/profile/                  @your-org/data-platform

# Incremental models are more complex and fragile. The README asks for them to
# be listed explicitly, because a selector cannot tell one from another.
# YOU: one line per incremental model, for example
# /models/marts/fct_events.sql  @your-org/data-platform

# The owner of a critical model directory should be the domain's data owner.
# YOU: one line per critical directory, for example
# /models/marts/finance/        @your-org/finance-data

# The anti-fraud gate itself. If the agent can change what judges it, it is
# judged by nothing. Gate rule G9 blocks the attempt on the agent's pull
# requests; this line is what makes a human read it on everyone else's.
/tools/                         @your-org/data-platform

# --- Two paths the README's table does not list ---

# On warehouses where the agent cannot read raw data, staging is effectively
# human-only work. Uncomment if that is your setup.
# /models/staging/              @your-org/data-platform

# Agent settings, if you use an agent that keeps them in the repository.
# Same reason as AGENTS.md: the agent should not write its own instructions.
# /.claude/                     @your-org/data-platform
